<article class="post-article"><a class="back-to-posts" href="/posts">← All posts</a> <header class="article-heading"><h1>SnapCane: making navigation tangible</h1> <p class="article-deck">What a self-steering cane taught me about fabrication, power, and designing around a person.</p> <p class="post-meta">Rohin Sood</p></header> <div class="prose"><p>SnapCane, also called Lighthouse, is a self-steering cane our team built for the Qualcomm Multiverse Hackathon. A motorized wheel at the tip turns a navigation decision into a physical nudge through the cane. The project brought together a phone, a cane-side computer, printed parts, and an actuator that actually had to touch the ground. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane">Project repository</a>.</p> <figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/main/Hardware/CAD/Assembly.png" alt="CAD rendering of the SnapCane assembly" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/><figcaption>Assembly rendering from the SnapCane repository: phone at the top, electronics and battery along the shaft, and the drive assembly at the tip. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/CAD/Assembly.png">Image source</a>.</figcaption></figure> <h2>What the hardware taught me</h2> <h3>A printable mechanism still needs a tolerance budget</h3> <p>The tip uses a six-roller, print-in-place omni wheel, adapted from an existing GrabCAD design for the motor shaft. Capturing the rollers inside the print reduces assembly work, but makes printer clearance part of the mechanism: a roller that fuses to its housing cannot do its job. The build documentation explicitly calls out fit checks and clearance adjustments. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/Bill%20of%20Materials.md">Hardware and original-design attribution</a>.</p> <p>My takeaway is to treat fabrication as part of design. A CAD assembly can describe the intended motion; the printed part tells you whether that motion is available.</p> <figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/main/Hardware/CAD/Omni%20Wheel.png" alt="CAD rendering of the print-in-place omni wheel" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer"/><figcaption>The wheel rendering from our repository. The underlying print-in-place design is credited to GrabCAD in the project's hardware documentation. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/CAD/Omni%20Wheel.png">Image source</a>.</figcaption></figure> <h3>Receiving a command is different from delivering power</h3> <p>The electronics have a particularly instructive failure mode. The Qwiic connection supplies logic power, but the geared motor needs a separate 12 V supply. Without motor power, commands can appear to succeed while the wheel does nothing. The assembly process therefore starts with the electronics on a desk, before enclosing them in the printed assembly. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/Assembly%20Instructions.md">Assembly guide</a>.</p> <p>That is a debugging habit I want to carry forward: follow the complete path from command to physical response. Check the supply, driver, connections, and load before assuming the problem is in the application.</p> <h3>Mounting is part of sensing</h3> <p>The phone cradle has a pivoting hinge, and the assembly guide includes camera-angle and ground-calibration checks. A mount affects what the camera sees, so its orientation belongs in the perception problem. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/Assembly%20Instructions.md">Assembly guide</a>.</p> <p>The next hardware questions are equally physical: how the weight feels over a long walk, how the battery behaves under sustained motor load, and how quickly a detected obstacle produces wheel torque. Those remain evaluation work, rather than results I can claim. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane/blob/main/Hardware/Bill%20of%20Materials.md">Documented next steps</a>.</p> <h2>The software that powers it</h2> <p>The documented perception architecture splits work between a Snapdragon phone and an Arduino UNO Q. The phone combines depth and walkability estimates into steering decisions; the board handles local sensing and actuation. Steering commitment reduces rapid left-right changes, while board-side hazard detection can override phone commands. Obstacle avoidance is designed to operate locally. The repository distinguishes the full perception system on <code>v3</code> from the merged demo on <code>main</code>. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane">Architecture and branch descriptions</a>.</p> <p>For me, the interesting connection is where these layers meet: a useful perception result has to become a stable, understandable physical action.</p> <p>SnapCane is a research prototype, not a validated replacement for a mobility aid. The repository calls for testing with a sighted companion and identifies user evaluation as future work. <a href="https://github.com/rohinsood/snap-cane#safety">Prototype limitations</a>.</p></div> <a class="back-to-posts article-end" href="/posts">← Back to all posts</a></article>

